1
00:00:00,530 --> 00:00:04,943
On May 24, 2013, three shipwreck hunters lowered a sonar into Lake Superior,

2
00:00:04,963 --> 00:00:07,510
about 30 miles north of Marquette, Michigan.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,590
20 minutes later, a shape appeared on their screen.

4
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,860
535 feet down.

5
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:17,140
A ship.

6
00:00:17,210 --> 00:00:19,075
Broken in two.

7
00:00:19,145 --> 00:00:23,350
She had been missing for almost exactly 100 years.

8
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:25,120
Her name was the Henry B.

9
00:00:25,190 --> 00:00:26,140
Smith.

10
00:00:26,210 --> 00:00:28,012
On a Sunday evening in November, 1913,

11
00:00:28,032 --> 00:00:32,183
she sailed out of Marquette into the deadliest storm in the history of the Great Lakes,

12
00:00:32,203 --> 00:00:33,190
with 25 men on board.

13
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:41,340
20 minutes after she left, the storm hit her with everything it had.

14
00:00:41,410 --> 00:00:44,460
And then she vanished.

15
00:00:44,530 --> 00:00:45,535
For 100 years,

16
00:00:45,555 --> 00:00:51,320
the best clues anyone had were some wreckage on a beach and a note in a bottle.

17
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:53,770
Both of them pointed the same way.

18
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:58,580
And when she was finally found, she was somewhere else entirely.

19
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:00,520
So let's work it out together.

20
00:01:00,590 --> 00:01:04,480
Why did her captain leave port in the middle of that storm?

21
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:08,050
What were her crew doing on deck as she sailed away?

22
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:10,810
Why did every clue point the wrong way?

23
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:14,470
And what does the wreck itself tell us about how she died?

24
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:18,210
By the end, you'll know more about the last night of the Henry B.

25
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:22,050
Smith than anyone did for a century.

26
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:26,370
If you came here from our Edmund Fitzgerald video, welcome.

27
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:31,470
This is the same lake, in the same month, 62 years earlier.

28
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:35,220
And if you'd like to hear the next story like this one, tap subscribe.

29
00:01:35,290 --> 00:01:41,320
It's how a small channel like this one finds the people who love these lakes.

30
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:45,640
The storm began on November 7, 1913.

31
00:01:45,710 --> 00:01:50,462
Two storm systems, one from the north and one that formed far to the south,

32
00:01:50,482 --> 00:01:52,690
came together over the Great Lakes.

33
00:01:53,410 --> 00:01:55,310
People gave it three names.

34
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:56,630
The Big Blow.

35
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:58,270
The Freshwater Fury.

36
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:00,530
And the White Hurricane.

37
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:07,580
For four days it threw gusts of up to 90 miles an hour and waves of more than 35 feet at every ship it could find.

38
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:11,490
Forecasters could see a storm coming.

39
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:14,990
What they couldn't see was how bad it would get.

40
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:19,523
In 1913, weather reports were collected only twice a day,

41
00:02:19,543 --> 00:02:22,040
and the maps were drawn by hand.

42
00:02:22,190 --> 00:02:27,840
By the time a forecast was ready, the weather it described had already changed.

43
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:31,043
On Lake Superior, the day before the Smith sailed,

44
00:02:31,063 --> 00:02:34,890
a single wave smashed into the wheelhouse of the freighter L.

45
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:35,860
C.

46
00:02:35,930 --> 00:02:36,300
Waldo.

47
00:02:36,370 --> 00:02:42,210
It tore three walls off the cabins below and threw her wheelsman out of the wheelhouse.

48
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:44,568
By the time the storm was over,

49
00:02:44,588 --> 00:02:49,260
more than 250 people were dead and 19 ships had been destroyed.

50
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:55,490
It is still the deadliest natural disaster in the history of the Great Lakes.

51
00:02:55,560 --> 00:03:00,620
And on Sunday, November 9, right in the middle of it, the Henry B.

52
00:03:00,690 --> 00:03:04,595
Smith was tied up in Marquette, waiting.

53
00:03:04,665 --> 00:03:07,075
She was a big ship for her time.

54
00:03:07,145 --> 00:03:10,339
545 feet long, built in 1906 at Lorain, Ohio,

55
00:03:10,359 --> 00:03:14,125
and named after a lumberman from Ludington, Michigan.

56
00:03:16,785 --> 00:03:21,545
She arrived in Marquette on November 6 to load iron ore.

57
00:03:21,615 --> 00:03:22,985
Then the cold came.

58
00:03:23,055 --> 00:03:26,277
The ore froze solid inside the railway cars,

59
00:03:26,297 --> 00:03:30,035
and men had to climb in and break it loose by hand.

60
00:03:30,725 --> 00:03:33,995
Every hour, the Smith fell further behind.

61
00:03:34,065 --> 00:03:37,895
For her captain, James Owen, it was the last thing he needed.

62
00:03:37,965 --> 00:03:40,135
It had been a bad year.

63
00:03:40,205 --> 00:03:45,005
One misfortune after another had left the Smith late, again and again.

64
00:03:45,075 --> 00:03:48,259
And there's a story, told in Marquette then and still told today,

65
00:03:48,279 --> 00:03:52,055
that her owners had made it very clear: this last trip had better be on time.

66
00:03:56,425 --> 00:03:57,855
We can't prove that.

67
00:03:57,925 --> 00:04:01,035
But the story has never gone away.

68
00:04:01,105 --> 00:04:04,925
Then, on Sunday afternoon, something changed.

69
00:04:04,995 --> 00:04:06,885
The wind dropped.

70
00:04:06,955 --> 00:04:10,535
By one account, it fell to about 32 miles an hour.

71
00:04:10,605 --> 00:04:14,475
The lake was still rolling, but for the first time in days,

72
00:04:14,495 --> 00:04:16,915
it looked like the storm was passing.

73
00:04:16,985 --> 00:04:21,195
At about five o'clock, the last car of ore was loaded.

74
00:04:21,265 --> 00:04:22,075
And the Henry B.

75
00:04:22,145 --> 00:04:24,305
Smith didn't wait.

76
00:04:24,375 --> 00:04:29,396
She backed away from the dock and headed straight out into the lake.

77
00:04:29,466 --> 00:04:31,886
People on the shore watched her go.

78
00:04:31,956 --> 00:04:35,166
And what they saw is the first real clue.

79
00:04:35,236 --> 00:04:41,246
Her deck was covered by 32 hatches, the openings down into her cargo holds.

80
00:04:41,316 --> 00:04:43,656
Before a ship heads into open water,

81
00:04:43,676 --> 00:04:47,196
every one of them has to be covered and fastened down.

82
00:04:47,266 --> 00:04:51,246
On a ship like the Smith, that normally took hours.

83
00:04:51,316 --> 00:04:53,161
As she steamed out of the harbour,

84
00:04:53,181 --> 00:04:56,396
the watchers could see her deckhands still working on them.

85
00:04:56,466 --> 00:05:01,166
20 minutes later, the storm came back with its full force.

86
00:05:01,236 --> 00:05:04,016
The lull hadn't been the end of the storm.

87
00:05:04,086 --> 00:05:05,846
It was a gap.

88
00:05:05,916 --> 00:05:08,272
That night, the second storm swung north into the first,

89
00:05:08,292 --> 00:05:10,181
and over Lake Superior and three other lakes,

90
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:12,896
the winds rose past 70 miles an hour and stayed there for hours.

91
00:05:17,506 --> 00:05:20,076
The last time anyone saw the Henry B.

92
00:05:20,146 --> 00:05:22,216
Smith, she was turning to port.

93
00:05:22,286 --> 00:05:23,716
Turning north.

94
00:05:23,786 --> 00:05:27,766
Maybe her captain was trying to reach the shelter of the Keweenaw Peninsula,

95
00:05:27,786 --> 00:05:30,766
the long arm of land that reaches out into Lake Superior.

96
00:05:31,666 --> 00:05:36,286
Then the snow closed in, and she was gone.

97
00:05:36,356 --> 00:05:37,696
Remember that turn.

98
00:05:37,766 --> 00:05:40,422
It matters later.

99
00:05:40,492 --> 00:05:45,502
2 days later, wreckage began washing up on the beaches east of Marquette.

100
00:05:45,572 --> 00:05:46,982
At Chocolay Bay.

101
00:05:47,052 --> 00:05:48,542
At Shot Point.

102
00:05:48,612 --> 00:05:51,362
At Laughing Fish Point.

103
00:05:51,432 --> 00:05:52,062
The Henry B.

104
00:05:52,132 --> 00:05:53,932
Smith never reached the Soo.

105
00:05:54,002 --> 00:06:00,982
Of her 25 men, only two were ever found, both of them far to the east.

106
00:06:01,342 --> 00:06:02,702
So where was she?

107
00:06:02,772 --> 00:06:06,252
The wreckage seemed to point east, along that shore.

108
00:06:06,322 --> 00:06:13,302
Then, in June 1914, 7 months later, someone found a note in a bottle.

109
00:06:13,562 --> 00:06:15,232
It claimed to be from the Henry B.

110
00:06:15,302 --> 00:06:15,612
Smith.

111
00:06:15,682 --> 00:06:21,922
And it said the ship had broken in two, 12 miles east of Marquette.

112
00:06:21,992 --> 00:06:23,352
East again.

113
00:06:23,422 --> 00:06:26,762
Two clues, both pointing the same way.

114
00:06:26,832 --> 00:06:28,912
But there was a problem with the note.

115
00:06:28,982 --> 00:06:35,602
It was dated November 12, and by then the Smith had already been gone for 2 days.

116
00:06:35,672 --> 00:06:40,182
After a long debate, her owners decided it was a fake.

117
00:06:40,252 --> 00:06:44,432
So before I tell you where she was really found, I want your guess.

118
00:06:44,502 --> 00:06:49,182
Was she east of Marquette, where the wreckage and the note pointed?

119
00:06:49,252 --> 00:06:50,672
Or somewhere else?

120
00:06:50,742 --> 00:06:52,742
Put it in the comments right now.

121
00:06:52,812 --> 00:06:55,122
East, or somewhere else.

122
00:06:55,192 --> 00:07:01,027
One word is enough, and in a few minutes, you'll find out if you were right.

123
00:07:01,097 --> 00:07:05,337
For 100 years, Lake Superior kept her secret.

124
00:07:05,407 --> 00:07:10,007
The ore boats kept sailing past Marquette, and they kept getting bigger.

125
00:07:10,077 --> 00:07:11,931
In 1975, 62 years after the Smith,

126
00:07:11,951 --> 00:07:16,175
the Edmund Fitzgerald went down further east on the same lake, broken in two,

127
00:07:16,195 --> 00:07:17,057
with all 29 men.

128
00:07:22,037 --> 00:07:24,307
And all that time, the Henry B.

129
00:07:24,377 --> 00:07:28,107
Smith lay somewhere in the dark, waiting.

130
00:07:28,177 --> 00:07:32,897
Lake Superior is the largest freshwater lake in the world by area.

131
00:07:32,967 --> 00:07:37,357
Finding one ship in it takes patience, money, and a lot of luck.

132
00:07:37,427 --> 00:07:38,122
Jerry Eliason,

133
00:07:38,142 --> 00:07:42,823
Ken Merryman and Kraig Smith had been hunting Lake Superior's lost ships for about 20 years,

134
00:07:42,843 --> 00:07:44,407
going out several times a year.

135
00:07:48,747 --> 00:07:50,557
And they didn't search blindly.

136
00:07:50,627 --> 00:07:51,996
Jerry and his wife, Karen,

137
00:07:52,016 --> 00:07:57,607
dug through government records and archives for anything that could narrow down where the Smith might be.

138
00:07:59,267 --> 00:08:03,437
Jerry's son, Jarrod, built the sonar they used.

139
00:08:03,507 --> 00:08:07,507
Exactly how they narrowed it down, they kept to themselves.

140
00:08:07,577 --> 00:08:11,863
They wanted to use the same method to find more ships.

141
00:08:11,933 --> 00:08:12,895
On May 24, 2013,

142
00:08:12,915 --> 00:08:18,913
they took their boat out to one small patch of Lake Superior and lowered the sonar into the water.

143
00:08:21,353 --> 00:08:24,933
20 minutes later, there she was.

144
00:08:25,003 --> 00:08:27,863
535 feet down.

145
00:08:27,933 --> 00:08:30,913
About 30 miles north of Marquette.

146
00:08:30,983 --> 00:08:31,723
North.

147
00:08:31,793 --> 00:08:32,923
Not east.

148
00:08:32,993 --> 00:08:36,672
Nowhere near the beaches where the wreckage washed up,

149
00:08:36,692 --> 00:08:39,823
and nowhere near where the note said she sank.

150
00:08:39,893 --> 00:08:43,343
If you guessed somewhere else, you were right.

151
00:08:43,413 --> 00:08:46,053
But they still couldn't prove it was her.

152
00:08:46,123 --> 00:08:52,103
So a month later, they came back and lowered a camera on a tether 500 feet long.

153
00:08:52,173 --> 00:08:54,893
It got caught on the wreck for half an hour.

154
00:08:54,963 --> 00:08:58,678
And in that time, it showed them everything.

155
00:08:58,748 --> 00:09:02,928
If you've been waiting for this moment, give the video a like.

156
00:09:02,998 --> 00:09:08,518
It tells YouTube that a story like this one is worth showing to more people.

157
00:09:08,588 --> 00:09:12,428
She is sitting upright on the bottom, mostly in one piece.

158
00:09:12,498 --> 00:09:18,188
Her pilothouse is still on her bow, and her forward mast is still standing.

159
00:09:18,258 --> 00:09:22,628
Her stern cabins have collapsed, crushed on the way down.

160
00:09:22,698 --> 00:09:27,908
And on her stern, after 100 years, you can still read her name.

161
00:09:27,978 --> 00:09:28,628
Henry B.

162
00:09:28,698 --> 00:09:30,113
Smith.

163
00:09:30,183 --> 00:09:33,118
But she is broken in two, near the middle.

164
00:09:33,188 --> 00:09:36,892
And all around her, spilled across the lake floor,

165
00:09:36,912 --> 00:09:40,168
is the iron ore she was carrying that night.

166
00:09:40,483 --> 00:09:43,218
Now think back to the clues.

167
00:09:43,288 --> 00:09:46,528
The note said she broke in two, and she did.

168
00:09:46,598 --> 00:09:52,188
But it said that happened 12 miles east of Marquette, and she's 30 miles north.

169
00:09:52,258 --> 00:09:56,518
Whoever wrote it guessed right about one thing and wrong about the other.

170
00:09:56,588 --> 00:10:00,644
And breaking in two is exactly what anyone who knew these long,

171
00:10:00,664 --> 00:10:02,908
heavy ore boats might have guessed.

172
00:10:02,978 --> 00:10:04,238
And the people on the shore?

173
00:10:04,308 --> 00:10:08,828
They said the last time they saw her, she was turning to port.

174
00:10:08,898 --> 00:10:10,048
Turning north.

175
00:10:10,118 --> 00:10:13,108
That's exactly the direction she was found in.

176
00:10:13,178 --> 00:10:14,278
They were right.

177
00:10:14,348 --> 00:10:16,878
She hadn't run east along the coast.

178
00:10:16,948 --> 00:10:23,438
She had turned north, maybe for the shelter of the Keweenaw, and she never made it.

179
00:10:23,508 --> 00:10:26,808
So why did the wreckage end up on the beaches to the east?

180
00:10:26,878 --> 00:10:30,908
Because floating wreckage goes wherever the wind and waves push it.

181
00:10:30,978 --> 00:10:34,080
That night the wind was blowing hard from the north and northwest,

182
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:37,958
and it would have driven anything floating toward the shore south and east of her.

183
00:10:40,358 --> 00:10:45,448
Toward Chocolay Bay, Shot Point and Laughing Fish Point.

184
00:10:45,518 --> 00:10:47,498
So what actually sank the Henry B.

185
00:10:47,568 --> 00:10:48,358
Smith?

186
00:10:48,428 --> 00:10:50,358
Nobody can say for certain.

187
00:10:50,428 --> 00:10:54,798
No one survived to tell us, and the wreck can't tell us everything.

188
00:10:54,868 --> 00:10:57,968
But the evidence points to three suspects.

189
00:10:58,038 --> 00:10:58,998
One.

190
00:10:59,068 --> 00:11:00,138
The hatches.

191
00:11:00,208 --> 00:11:04,928
When she left Marquette, her crew were still covering 32 hatches,

192
00:11:04,948 --> 00:11:07,188
a job that normally took hours.

193
00:11:07,448 --> 00:11:10,528
20 minutes later, the full storm hit her.

194
00:11:10,598 --> 00:11:13,166
If even a few of them weren't fastened down,

195
00:11:13,186 --> 00:11:17,578
every wave that broke over her deck would have poured water into her holds.

196
00:11:18,398 --> 00:11:19,238
Two.

197
00:11:19,308 --> 00:11:20,828
The storm itself.

198
00:11:20,898 --> 00:11:25,618
That night the winds over Lake Superior reached hurricane force.

199
00:11:25,688 --> 00:11:28,076
A ship 545 feet long, loaded with iron ore,

200
00:11:28,096 --> 00:11:32,668
can be lifted at both ends by waves like that, with nothing holding up her middle.

201
00:11:36,818 --> 00:11:39,128
And she broke near the middle.

202
00:11:39,198 --> 00:11:40,048
Three.

203
00:11:40,118 --> 00:11:41,228
The decision.

204
00:11:41,298 --> 00:11:44,298
Captain Owen had been waiting for 3 days.

205
00:11:44,368 --> 00:11:47,348
He was behind schedule, and maybe under pressure.

206
00:11:47,418 --> 00:11:52,418
Then the storm went quiet, just for a moment, and he took the chance.

207
00:11:52,488 --> 00:11:55,958
20 minutes later, it was gone.

208
00:11:56,028 --> 00:11:57,888
And here's the hardest part.

209
00:11:57,958 --> 00:12:01,808
Did she break in two on the surface, which would point to the storm?

210
00:12:01,878 --> 00:12:05,795
Or did water through the hatches drag her down first,

211
00:12:05,815 --> 00:12:08,618
and she broke when she hit the bottom?

212
00:12:08,688 --> 00:12:12,838
It's the same question people still ask about the Edmund Fitzgerald.

213
00:12:12,908 --> 00:12:18,362
And just like the Fitzgerald, nobody has been able to settle it.

214
00:12:18,432 --> 00:12:19,662
So now it's your turn.

215
00:12:19,732 --> 00:12:21,762
You've seen all the evidence.

216
00:12:21,832 --> 00:12:23,102
What sank the Henry B.

217
00:12:23,172 --> 00:12:24,122
Smith?

218
00:12:24,192 --> 00:12:25,062
The hatches?

219
00:12:25,132 --> 00:12:26,272
The storm?

220
00:12:26,342 --> 00:12:27,962
The decision to sail?

221
00:12:28,032 --> 00:12:32,212
Or, like so many wrecks on this lake, all three at once?

222
00:12:32,282 --> 00:12:34,192
Tell me in the comments.

223
00:12:34,262 --> 00:12:36,512
I read every single one.

224
00:12:36,582 --> 00:12:40,282
25 men sailed out of Marquette that Sunday evening.

225
00:12:40,352 --> 00:12:42,432
Only two were ever found.

226
00:12:42,502 --> 00:12:46,552
Somewhere 535 feet down, the Henry B.

227
00:12:46,622 --> 00:12:53,602
Smith is a grave for the rest, and for 100 years, nobody even knew where it was.

228
00:12:54,222 --> 00:12:56,172
And she isn't the only one.

229
00:12:56,242 --> 00:13:00,852
Lake Superior still holds ships that have never been found.

230
00:13:00,922 --> 00:13:05,512
If you want to hear their stories too, subscribe and turn on the bell.

231
00:13:05,582 --> 00:13:10,712
And if you haven't seen it yet, our video on the Edmund Fitzgerald is right here.

232
00:13:10,782 --> 00:13:16,212
Same lake, same kind of storm, and the same question nobody can answer.

233
00:13:16,282 --> 00:13:09,362
Thanks for watching.
